]

So ArrowLeft keydown had defaultPrevented:true, meaning something prevented default action (likely the datepicker's keyboard handler intercepting ArrowLeft to adjust calendar). Since the calendar is visible (maybe open?), the ArrowLeft is being captured by pikaday to navigate dates, preventing caret movement in the input.

Thus we need to modify pikaday.js such that when the picker is bound to a field (or when container is set but bound:false?), the keyboard listeners should not interfere when focus is in another input (like #notes). Currently pikaday adds a global keydown listener on document when opts.keyboardInput is true (default true). In the code we saw:

if (opts.keyboardInput) {
    addEvent(document, 'keydown', self._onKeyChange);
}

Thus any keydown anywhere triggers _onKeyChange, which checks if self.isVisible() and then handles arrow keys to adjust date.

We need to ensure that keyboard navigation only affects the datepicker when the picker is bound to the field (i.e., the associated input) OR when the picker itself has focus? Usually datepickers should only respond to keyboard when the associated input is focused or the picker is focused. In this usage, bound:false, container set, field: #delivery-date. So the datepicker is attached to the delivery-date field but not bound (so not automatically shown/hidden on focus). It is rendered into container #calendar and always visible? Actually they call new Pikaday with field and container, bound:false. The picker likely shows immediately? In constructor, if opts.bound then this.hide(); else this.show(); Since bound:false, they call this.show() at end. So the picker is always visible.

Thus keyboard listener globally catches ArrowLeft even when focus is in #notes, causing date change instead of caret move.

Fix: modify _onKeyChange to check that the event target is either the field or an element within the picker (maybe the container) OR that the picker is bound and the field is active? Simpler: only react if the event.target is the field (or a descendant of the picker?) But we want to allow navigation when the picker has focus (like when user tabs into the picker buttons). However the test expects ArrowLeft in another text field still moves caret, so we need to ignore global keydown when focus is not inside the picker or its associated field.

Implementation: In _onKeyChange, after checking self.isVisible(), also check if document.activeElement is either the field or within the picker container (self.el). If not, return early (do nothing). This will allow ArrowLeft to work elsewhere.

Alternatively, we could attach keydown listener to the field instead of document when bound:false? But the spec says keyboardInput enables keyboard navigation for the picker; typical approach is to listen on the field when bound, else maybe on the picker container. We'll adjust.

